KIGALI, October 2 (IPS)  - Post-harvest losses, particularly in rural areas, threaten the gains Africa is making in agricultural production and export capacity.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44242&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Renewables, Farmer Aggregation Offer Solutions to Africa’s Post-Harvest Losses”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44241</id><title>For Pakistan Rising Fistula Cases Make SDG 3 Goal Elusive</title><updated>2026-10-02T08:10:37-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44241" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/main-fistula-scaled-e1790927431938.png" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/main-fistula-scaled-e1790927431938.png" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/main-fistula-scaled-e1790927431938.png" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/main-fistula-scaled-e1790927431938.png&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
KARACHI, Pakistan, October 2 (IPS)  - “You’re living a life drained of joy,” is how Shehnaz Sher Ali describes her “perpetually wet and stinky” existence. “I wouldn’t wish it on my worst enemy,” says the 35-year-old, who travelled by bus for more than 12 hours with her husband from their village in Rahim Yar Khan, Punjab, changing diapers whenever possible to keep the smell from reaching the fellow passengers.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44241&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “For Pakistan Rising Fistula Cases Make SDG 3 Goal Elusive”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44240</id><title>SUDAN: ‘It’s Shocking to Be Forced into Silence Simply Because You Are a Refugee’</title><updated>2026-10-02T07:32:45-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44240" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/anonymous_021026-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;CIVICUS discusses life as a refugee with an exiled Sudanese civil society activist who has asked to remain anonymous for security reasons.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44240&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “SUDAN: ‘It’s Shocking to Be Forced into Silence Simply Because You Are a Refugee’”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44239</id><title>The Ingenuity Trap</title><updated>2026-10-02T05:54:48-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44239" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Mariam-Daher_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Mariam-Daher_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Mariam-Daher_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Mariam-Daher_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
DOHA, Qatar, October 2 (IPS)  - In the 21st century, getting a pencil into Gaza became international news. In January, after more than two years of restrictions, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.rte.ie/news/middle-east/2026/0127/1555329-gaza-israel/&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;UNICEF finally brought in learning kits with pencils and exercise books&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/9/20/back-to-school-in-gaza-learning-in-tents-after-years-of-disruption&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;Textbooks stayed out&lt;/a&gt;. By then, the UN had recorded damage to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.un.org/unispal/document/unicef-humanitarian-action-appeal-for-children-2026-state-of-palestine-revision-1-february-2026/&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;97.5 per cent of Gaza’s schools&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44239&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The Ingenuity Trap”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44238</id><title>If We Can Start an Epoch, We Can Start a Farming Revolution</title><updated>2026-10-02T05:01:32-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44238" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Members-of-Smallholder_01.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Members-of-Smallholder_01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Members-of-Smallholder_01-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Members-of-Smallholder_01.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
NEW YORK, October 2 (IPS)  - In November 1952, the United States detonated the world’s first thermonuclear device at Enewetak Atoll in the Marshall Islands. The test, code-named Ivy Mike, erased the island of Elugelab and scattered a signature of plutonium across the planet that remains in lake beds, ice sheets and ocean sediments today.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44238&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “If We Can Start an Epoch, We Can Start a Farming Revolution”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44245</id><title>Escalating fighting in Yemen deepens food security crisis</title><updated>2026-10-02T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44245" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Renewed conflict in Yemen has triggered a surge in hunger that could have consequences in the wider region and beyond, the World Food Programme (WFP) warned on Friday. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44245&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Escalating fighting in Yemen deepens food security crisis”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44244</id><title>Gaza: WHO appeals for massive aid influx ahead of winter</title><updated>2026-10-02T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44244" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/13-07-2026_UNNews_Gaza_Daily_Life_05.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/13-07-2026_UNNews_Gaza_Daily_Life_05.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/13-07-2026_UNNews_Gaza_Daily_Life_05.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/07/13-07-2026_UNNews_Gaza_Daily_Life_05.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Repeated delays in bringing lifesaving medicines, prosthetics and more from Israel into Gaza risk having devastating consequences on Palestinians who urgently need them in the war-torn enclave, the UN World Health Organization (WHO) said on Friday. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44244&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Gaza: WHO appeals for massive aid influx ahead of winter”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44243</id><title>‘I have not lost my dreams’: Refugee education funding cuts threaten millions</title><updated>2026-10-02T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44243" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/02-10-2026_UNHCR_Ethiopia_RefugeeEducation.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/02-10-2026_UNHCR_Ethiopia_RefugeeEducation.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/02-10-2026_UNHCR_Ethiopia_RefugeeEducation.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/02-10-2026_UNHCR_Ethiopia_RefugeeEducation.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A teenage girl who fled war-torn Sudan for neighbouring Egypt and hopes to pursue a technology degree is an example of why countries must ensure that young refugees can continue to learn.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/02/44243&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘I have not lost my dreams’: Refugee education funding cuts threaten millions”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/01/44231</id><title>Caring for Carers: Addressing Workplace Violence Against Nurses in Malaysia</title><updated>2026-10-01T16:17:36-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/01/44231" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/cafeforcarers.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/cafeforcarers.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/cafeforcarers-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/cafeforcarers.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
KUALA LUMPUR, October 1 (IPS)  - Nurses are often called the backbone of the health system. We praise their compassion, commitment and resilience. On Nurses’ Day we thank them. During emergencies and crises, we call them heroes. But there is a harder question Malaysia, like most countries around the world, needs to ask more seriously: what happens when the people who care for everyone else are not safe, supported or respected at work?
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/01/44231&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Caring for Carers: Addressing Workplace Violence Against Nurses in Malaysia”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/01/44229</id><title>Afghanistan’s Drought Deepens, and Women Bear the Heaviest Burden</title><updated>2026-10-01T14:36:25-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/01/44229" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/afghanistandrought1.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/afghanistandrought1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/afghanistandrought1-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/afghanistandrought1.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
KABUL, October 1 (IPS)  - Drought has depleted wells, withered fruit trees, and reduced crop yields across Afghanistan. The heaviest burden of the water shortage falls on women in their daily lives, as they must fetch water from increasingly distant sources and use every single drop with extreme care.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/01/44229&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Afghanistan’s Drought Deepens, and Women Bear the Heaviest Burden”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0008s ch:1 -->